        "dependency_level": 11,
        "title": "Effective synthesis of fixed-dimensional unitaries",
        "justified_by": [],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John Watrous, The Theory of Quantum Information",
              "url": "https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~watrous/TQI/TQI.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapter 3, unitary decompositions and finite-dimensional circuit constructions"
            },
            {
              "title": "Andrew M. Childs, Lecture Notes on Quantum Algorithms",
              "url": "https://www.cs.umd.edu/~amchilds/qa/qa.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapter 2, PDF pp. 15–18; fixed-width synthesis and gate approximation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Fix a nonnegative integer $q$ and dimension $D=2^q$, independent of input length. Given a $D\\times D$ unitary whose entries have polynomial-time Cauchy names from a finite description and a rational $0<\\eta<1/2$, a deterministic polynomial-time procedure outputs a circuit on its $q$ data wires, with a constant number of clean ancillas returned to zero, for which there is a unit scalar $\\lambda$ with $\\|V-\\lambda U\\|\\le\\eta$. The procedure has polynomial bit cost and polynomial gate count in the description length and $1/\\eta$. Scalar phase is immaterial only for this unconditional circuit. The construction uses complex Givens elimination with robust pivoting, a constant-width decomposition into unconditional one-qubit $SU(2)$ rotations and CNOT gates, and the finite-precision compiler of [[thm-qc-solovay-kitaev-bit-complexity]]. Its exact reversible-control subconstruction gives Toffoli from $H,T,T^\\dagger,\\mathrm{CNOT}$ by the parity-phase identity in the proof.",
        "axiom_use": "None; the decomposition is a finite constructive matrix algorithm."
      },
