        "dependency_level": 15,
        "title": "Parallel QMA amplification remains sound for entangled witnesses",
        "justified_by": [],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John Watrous, Quantum Computational Complexity",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0804.3401",
              "locator": "§V.3, weak error reduction, PDF pp. 21–23"
            },
            {
              "title": "Dorit Aharonov and Tomer Naveh, Quantum NP: A Survey",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0210077",
              "locator": "§2, QMA amplification discussion, PDF pp. 4–7"
            },
            {
              "title": "Ronald de Wolf, Quantum Computing: Lecture Notes",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.09415",
              "locator": "Chapter 14, §14.1, PDF pp. 116–117"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let a QMA verifier have completeness $c$ and soundness $s$ with $0\\le s<c\\le1$, and put $\\gamma=c-s$. For $0<\\delta<1$, choose an integer\n\n$$r\\ge 2\\gamma^{-2}\\ln(1/\\delta).$$\n\nThen the parallel verifier of [[alg-qa-33-qma-parallel-majority-amplification]] has completeness at least $1-\\delta$ and soundness at most $\\delta$. On a NO input its soundness bound holds for every joint $r$-block witness state, including entangled states. The repetition uses $r$ witness blocks and $r$ verifier copies, so choosing the least integer $r$ satisfying the bound gives polynomial size when $\\gamma^{-1}$ and $\\ln(1/\\delta)$ are polynomially bounded. The probability bounds hold for every larger $r$ as well.",
        "axiom_use": "None; the proof uses finite tensor products, finite POVMs, and a choice-free operator moment bound."
      },
